{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cfm8Nbni2TpctNmU6hWZz","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01MenFwEtgwLbbzBr5eoUxLJ","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"C=/scratch/users/kushinm/auto-psych/outer_loop_live/run3/data/subjective_randomness/experiment3/model_loop/iter_3/candidate_0_repair_1\ncat << 'EOF' > $C/hypothesis.md\nPeople do not judge the two sequences of a pair in isolation: the pair as a whole sets a shared reference — how ordinary the two look, on average, for a fair coin of that length (how typical their heads/tails balance and number of switches are) — and that reference sets how sharply the difference in randomness between them is felt. Beside an equally odd-looking partner (two streaky or lopsided sequences) the difference stands out by contrast and is acted on decisively, while between two ordinary-looking sequences the same difference is felt only weakly, so the same sequence is chosen more or less decisively depending on its partner; each sequence's randomness is otherwise judged as in the current best account.\nEOF\necho pair_gist_contrast_gain > $C/model_name.txt\ncd $C && /scratch/users/kushinm/auto-psych/outer_loop_live/venv/bin/python - << 'EOF'\nsrc = open(\"candidate.py\").read()\nold_doc_end = src.index('\"\"\"', 3) + 3\nsrc = src.replace(\"\"\"(how typical their heads/tails balance and switch count are under a fair coin,\nper flip, averaged over the pair)\"\"\", \"\"\"(how typical their heads/tails balance and switch count are for a fair coin\nof that length, standardised within length against the fair coin's own\ndistribution so the reference does not merely track length, averaged over\nthe pair)\"\"\")\nsrc = src.replace(\"\"\"randomness evidence, z_pair the centred pair typicality (fixed constants, so\nthe gain means the same on every stimulus set)\"\"\", \"\"\"randomness evidence, z_pair the pair's mean within-length typicality z-score\n(exact fair-coin constant